add this to records:

v=spf1 ip4:YOUR_SERVER_IP ~all












To build a lightweight notification system in C that hooks into GitHub webhook events and captures emails from your website, you'll want a small backend service running on your server alongside NGINX. Since you're already writing C web servers (like spyderfly) and managing C applications, you can write the entire backend listener and webhook processor in C using a fastcgi setup or a direct HTTP socket handler, paired with a simple frontend form.

Here is how you can implement this end-to-end, broken down into the website form, the backend C receiver for emails and webhooks, and the email-sending mechanism.

1. The Website Frontend (HTML & JavaScript)
Place this form snippet wherever you want the subscription box to appear on your site. It uses a simple fetch() request to send the email to your C backend endpoint (/api/subscribe).

HTML
<div class="newsletter-box">
    <h3>Get GitHub Updates</h3>
    <p>Would you like to receive email updates when the repository gets updated?</p>
    <form id="subForm">
        <input type="email" id="subscriberEmail" placeholder="your@email.com" required>
        <button type="submit">Subscribe</button>
    </form>
    <p id="subResponse" style="font-size: 0.9em; margin-top: 8px;"></p>
</div>

<script>
document.getElementById('subForm').addEventListener('submit', async function(e) {
    e.preventDefault();
    const email = document.getElementById('subscriberEmail').value;
    const responseBox = document.getElementById('subResponse');

    try {
        const response = await fetch('/api/subscribe', {
            method: 'POST',
            headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
            body: JSON.stringify({ email: email })
        });

        if (response.ok) {
            responseBox.style.color = 'green';
            responseBox.textContent = 'Successfully subscribed!';
            document.getElementById('subForm').reset();
        } else {
            responseBox.style.color = 'red';
            responseBox.textContent = 'Subscription failed. Try again later.';
        }
    } catch (err) {
        responseBox.style.color = 'red';
        responseBox.textContent = 'Network error occurred.';
    }
});
</script>
2. The C Backend Application
You can set up your C application to handle two distinct routes via an HTTP server or a FastCGI handler behind NGINX:

POST /api/subscribe — Validates and saves the subscriber's email to a flat file or local database (e.g., subscribers.txt).

POST /api/webhook — Listens for GitHub's JSON payload when a push event occurs, parses it, and triggers an email broadcast to all saved addresses.

Here is a conceptual C program demonstrating how you'd process the email storage and trigger notifications using standard system commands (like sendmail or curl) or a local SMTP pipeline:

C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

#define SUBSCRIBER_FILE "/var/lib/mysite/subscribers.txt"

// Function to save a new subscriber email
int save_subscriber(const char *email) {
    // Basic validation check
    if (strchr(email, '@') == NULL || strlen(email) > 254) {
        return -1;
    }

    // Check for duplicates
    FILE *file = fopen(SUBSCRIBER_FILE, "r");
    if (file) {
        char line[256];
        while (fgets(line, sizeof(line), file)) {
            line[strcspn(line, "\r\n")] = 0;
            if (strcmp(line, email) == 0) {
                fclose(file);
                return 0; // Already subscribed
            }
        }
        fclose(file);
    }

    // Append new email
    file = fopen(SUBSCRIBER_FILE, "a");
    if (!file) return -1;
    
    fprintf(file, "%s\n", email);
    fclose(file);
    return 1;
}

// Function to broadcast repository updates to all subscribers
void send_notifications(const char *repo_name, const char *commit_msg) {
    FILE *file = fopen(SUBSCRIBER_FILE, "r");
    if (!file) return;

    char email[256];
    char command[1024];

    while (fgets(email, sizeof(email), file)) {
        email[strcspn(email, "\r\n")] = 0;
        if (strlen(email) == 0) continue;

        // Construct a mail command using local sendmail/postfix
        // Alternatively, use libcurl to hit a transactional email API (SendGrid, Mailgun, etc.)
        snprintf(command, sizeof(command), 
                 "echo -e \"Subject: Update on %s\\n\\nNew commit: %s\" | sendmail %s", 
                 repo_name, commit_msg, email);
        
        int ret = system(command);
        if (ret != 0) {
            fprintf(stderr, "Failed to send email to %s\n", email);
        }
    }
    fclose(file);
}

int main() {
    // Example simulation of handling a subscription request
    // In your actual server loop, you'd parse the HTTP POST body for JSON: {"email":"user@example.com"}
    const char *new_user = "testuser@example.com";
    int res = save_subscriber(new_user);
    if (res == 1) {
        printf("Added new subscriber: %s\n", new_user);
    }

    // Example simulation of handling a GitHub Webhook payload
    // GitHub sends a JSON payload on push; your C parser extracts the repo name and commit message.
    send_notifications("sizablesplash/repo", "Fix memory leak in socket handler");

    return 0;
}
3. Configuring NGINX to Route Traffic
To tie the frontend JavaScript and GitHub webhooks into your C backend, configure NGINX to proxy those specific API paths to your running C daemon or FastCGI application:

Nginx
server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name yourdomain.com;

    # Your static website files root
    root /var/www/html;
    index index.html;

    # Route subscription requests to your C backend (assuming it listens on localhost:8080)
    location /api/subscribe {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8080/subscribe;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
    }

    # Route GitHub webhooks to your C backend
    location /api/webhook {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8080/webhook;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-GitHub-Event $http_x_github_event;
    }
}
Next Steps for Implementation
JSON Parsing in C: Since GitHub webhooks and your website frontend communicate via JSON, you can link a lightweight C JSON parser like cJSON into your project to easily extract fields like email or head_commit.message.

Mail Delivery: If you don't want to run a local MTA (like Postfix/sendmail) on your server, you can swap the sendmail shell command in the C code to use libcurl for making a POST request to an external transactional email provider's API.
